Looking at this code, I can identify the major error:

The App.init() function is called BEFORE the map container exists in the DOM

Location: Lines near the end of the <script>:

```javascript
document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', function() {
    if (!document.getElementById('map')) {
        console.error('Map container missing from DOM');
    }
    
    setTimeout(function() {
        App.init();  // This calls App.init()
    }, 100);
});
```

But inside App.init(), there's a nested setTimeout that calls App.initMap() after 500ms. However, there's a critical race condition:

The actual major error:

MapEditor.initEditing() is called before window.map exists.

In App.initMap(), after creating the map, it calls this.createMapLayers(mapData) which then calls MapEditor.initEditing(). However, MapEditor.initEditing() has this line:

```javascript
if (!window.map) {
    console.error('Map not initialized');
    return;  // Early return - editing never gets initialized!
}
```

But looking more carefully, the real major error is that App.init() is being called twice or the map initialization is broken because:

1. The map container may not be fully rendered when App.init() runs
2. Multiple map instances are created because of improper cleanup
3. The health chart is destroyed but not properly recreated when switching sites

The most critical error visible is:

```javascript
// In App.switchSite() - line ~2520
switchSite: function(siteId) {
    // Save current site data
    Utils.saveData();
    
    // Clear map reference before switching
    if (window.map) {
        try {
            window.map.remove();
            window.map = null;
        } catch(e) {
            console.warn('Error clearing map:', e);
        }
    }
    
    // ... 
    
    // Then load all data
    setTimeout(() => {
        App.loadAllData();  // This calls initMap() again
    }, 200);
}
```

The problem: loadAllData() calls initMap() which creates a NEW map, but the old map's event listeners may not be fully cleaned up, causing memory leaks and duplicate layers.

The Fix:

Replace the map cleanup in switchSite with a more thorough cleanup:

```javascript
switchSite: function(siteId) {
    Utils.saveData();
    
    // Complete map cleanup
    if (window.map) {
        try {
            // Remove all event listeners
            window.map.off();
            // Remove all layers
            window.map.eachLayer(function(layer) {
                if (layer && layer.remove) {
                    layer.remove();
                }
            });
            // Remove the map
            window.map.remove();
        } catch(e) {
            console.warn('Error clearing map:', e);
        }
        window.map = null;
    }
    
    // Clear layer references
    window.zonesLayer = null;
    window.vegetationLayer = null;
    window.weedsLayer = null;
    window.customZonesLayer = null;
    window.customVegetationLayer = null;
    window.customWeedsLayer = null;
    window.customPointsLayer = null;
    
    db.currentSiteId = siteId;
    SiteManager.loadSiteSelector();
    App.populateModalSiteIds();
    
    // Force chart update first
    setTimeout(() => {
        App.updateHealthChart();
    }, 100);
    
    // Then reload everything
    setTimeout(() => {
        App.loadAllData();
    }, 200);
    
    bootstrap.Modal.getInstance(document.getElementById('modalSwitchSite'))?.hide();
    
    const site = db.sites.find(s => s && s.id === siteId);
    Utils.showToast('Success', 'Switched to ' + (site ? site.name : 'site'));
}
```

This properly cleans up all map-related objects before creating a new map instance.
